  "query_preview": "/SATURDAY NIGHT THROUGH THURSDAY/...\nIssued at 1211 PM CST Thu Nov 26 2026\n\nSaturday Night-Sunday...Rain chances (70-90%) return for \nSaturday night as a shortwave trough digs into the Central \nPlains. A low pressure across the Mid-Mississippi River Valley \nwill deepen and support low-level frontogenesis along a \ndeformation zone over Iowa. With PWATs climbing close to 0.75 \ninches (70-90% chance) ahead of this system with the moisture \nadvection from a Pacific atmospheric river and Gulf of America, \na large shield of stratiform rain is forecasted to develop. \nLatest LREF guidance is favoring rainfall amounts between \n0.2-0.5 inches (60-80% chance) through Sunday. This system \nquickly moves off to the east as a weak high pressure settles \nSunday night.\n\nMonday-Thursday...Mostly zonal flow sets in for next week with \nsubtle shortwave passages pushing weak cold fronts through the \narea with limited precipitation chances. With no real air mass \nchanges either, the one left after Sunday's system should slowly\nmodify throughout the week as temperatures rise a few degrees \neach day. By the middle of next week, highs could return to the \n50s for the start of December (50-80% per LREF guidance).",
